      "content": "<h3>Benefits and advantages</h3>\n<p>A <code>Stack</code> follows one simple rule: <strong>last in, first out</strong> (LIFO). Think of a pile of plates: you put a plate on top and you take the top plate first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Very easy to learn.</strong> Only a few methods matter: <code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, <code>peek</code>, <code>empty</code>, <code>search</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Perfect for \"undo\" thinking.</strong> The most recent thing is always the first to come back: undo, back button, nested brackets, function calls.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast core operations.</strong> <code>push</code>, <code>pop</code> and <code>peek</code> work at the top and cost O(1) (amortized for push).</li>\n<li><strong>It is also a Vector.</strong> So you can still use <code>get(i)</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>size</code> and loops over it.</li>\n<li><strong>Thread-safe methods</strong> (inherited from Vector, every call is synchronized).</li>\n<li><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> stacks turn recursion into loops (depth-first search, expression evaluation, parsing) and avoid <code>StackOverflowError</code> on deep inputs because the explicit stack lives on the heap.</li>\n</ul>"
