      "content": "<h3>Disadvantages and when NOT to use it</h3>\n<p>The <code>Stack</code> class has a good idea but an old design. Java itself recommends something else for new code.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It is a legacy class.</strong> The Javadoc says to prefer <code>Deque</code> (for example <code>ArrayDeque</code>) for LIFO work.</li>\n<li><strong>It exposes too much.</strong> Because it extends Vector, anyone can call <code>add(0, x)</code> or <code>remove(2)</code> and break the stack idea.</li>\n<li><strong>Slower.</strong> Each call is synchronized even when you use one thread.</li>\n<li><strong>Strange order.</strong> Printing a Stack shows bottom to top, and iteration also goes bottom to top, the opposite of the order <code>pop</code> returns. An <code>ArrayDeque</code> used as a stack iterates top to bottom.</li>\n<li><strong>Pop on empty throws.</strong> <code>pop()</code> and <code>peek()</code> throw <code>EmptyStackException</code> when nothing is there; always check <code>empty()</code> first.</li>\n<li><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> use <code>Deque&lt;E&gt; stack = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;()</code> with <code>push/pop/peek</code>. It is faster, has no locks, and its interface only allows stack-like operations when you type the variable as <code>Deque</code>. It does not allow null elements.</li>\n</ul>"
